.TH EUCLID-CLI-ENS-SET-TOPIC-RETENTION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ens-set-topic-retention \- set how long a topic keeps what is published to it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ens set-topic-retention
.BR \-\-topic | \-t
.I NAME
.BR \-\-retention\-period | \-r
.I DURATION
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets how long messages published to a topic are kept.
.PP
A topic is not consumed the way a queue is: a publish fans the message
out to the subscriptions there and then, and what stays behind is the
record of what was published. Nothing was ever going to remove it, so
without a retention period a topic grows without limit \- and since
every topic shares one collection, that cost is paid by every publish in
the installation rather than by the topic that collected it.
.SH WHEN IT TAKES EFFECT
For messages published afterwards. The ones already stored keep the
expiry they were given when they were published.
.PP
That is deliberate: the expiry is a date on each message and the
database removes it from there, so shortening a busy topic's retention
would otherwise mean rewriting every message it holds \- and lengthening
it could not bring back what has already gone. To clear out what a topic
has now, use
.BR euclid-cli-ens-purge-topic (1).
.SH THE DEFAULT
A topic starts with no period of its own, which is written as
.IR 0 ,
and follows
.I euclid.modules.ens.retention-period
\- fourteen days unless the installation says otherwise. Setting a topic
back to
.I 0
returns it to following that, including any later change to it.
.PP
Longer than a queue message's four days, because the two are different
things: a queue message is consumed and gone, and this is a record of
what was sent.
.SH KEEPING EVERYTHING
A period of
.I \-1
\- also spelled
.I forever
\- keeps every message published to the topic.
.PP
It is not a very long period. The message is stored with no expiry at
all, which is what a message published before retention existed looks
like and what the database's TTL index ignores, so nothing is ever going
to come along and remove it. That also means the topic grows without
limit, and since every topic shares one collection, that growth is paid
for by every publish in the installation. Only
.BR euclid-cli-ens-purge-topic (1)
empties it again.
.PP
A topic set to
.I \-1
has made a decision, so it no longer follows
.I euclid.modules.ens.retention-period
\- in either direction. An installation may set that setting to
.I \-1
as well, which makes keeping everything the default for topics that have
no period of their own.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-topic " " \fINAME\fR
Topic name. A full ERN also works, and is what reaches another
namespace. Required.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-retention\-period " " \fIDURATION\fR
How long to keep a published message: a number of seconds, or a number
with a unit \- \fIs\fR, \fIm\fR, \fIh\fR or \fId\fR, as in \fI14d\fR,
\fI36h\fR or \fI90m\fR. \fI0\fR gives the topic no period of its own and
follows the installation's. \fI\-1\fR, or the word \fIforever\fR, keeps
every message. A unit on \fI\-1\fR is not accepted \- \fI\-1d\fR is
neither a duration nor forever. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The period was set; the topic's ERN and its new period are printed.
.TP
.B 1
No topic of that name, the period was below \fI\-1\fR, or it was neither
a number of seconds nor a duration this understands.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Three days, for a topic whose history is only of short-term interest
euclid-cli ens set-topic-retention --topic orders --retention-period 3d

# The same thing in seconds
euclid-cli ens set-topic-retention --topic orders --retention-period 259200

# Back to following the installation's default
euclid-cli ens set-topic-retention --topic orders --retention-period 0

# Keep everything published here, for an audit trail nothing may remove
euclid-cli ens set-topic-retention --topic audit --retention-period forever
euclid-cli ens set-topic-retention --topic audit --retention-period -1
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ens (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-create-topic (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-publish-message (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-purge-topic (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-get-topic-metadata (1)
